/// docs/agents/guides/deeplinks.md
private let guideDeeplinks = #"""
# Open the app's links and routes

Use this guide to get the app straight to a screen: with a link the app already handles, a launch argument, or the project's own script. The project describes each way in once, as a **route** under `deeplinks:` in `.xcdev.yml`, and `xcd open` delivers it. It works on simulators and on Mac apps.

## Use the routes that are there

1. `xcd open` lists the project's routes with their parameters.
2. `xcd open <route> [name=value …]` opens one. Quote a value that has spaces: `xcd open item "title=Two Words"`.
3. `xcd open '<url>'` sends a link that no route describes, exactly as given. Quote a link that has `?` or `&`.

```sh
xcd run                         # `xcd open` never builds or installs
xcd open                        # the routes and their parameters
xcd open item id=42 tab=notes
xcd open item id=42 --dry-run   # prints what would be sent, and sends nothing
```

The app is started if it is not running. The result says what was sent and what became of the app:

```
opened item  device=iphone (iPhone 18 Pro, iOS 27.0)  app=running  pid=4312
url: myapp://host/item?id=42&tab=notes
check: label~Item 42 appeared after 0.6s
```

`app=` is `running` (the link went to the copy that was up), `started`, `restarted`, `started-by-link` (`--cold`), or `untouched` (a script route). xcd watches the app for a few seconds after the delivery, and a crash is reported as `app_crashed` with the crash report.

Use `xcd open` for the app's own links, not `xcd sim open-url`. `xcd open` makes sure the app is running, refuses a scheme the built app does not register, approves the scheme so that no "Open in …?" alert holds the link, and watches for a crash. `xcd sim open-url` is for a link meant for another app, such as a page in Safari.

## Find how the app takes links

Look in the app's source for these:

| Look for | It tells you | Route kind |
|---|---|---|
| `CFBundleURLTypes` in Info.plist, or URL Types in the target's settings | The custom schemes the app registers, like `myapp`. | `url` |
| `.onOpenURL`, `application(_:open:options:)`, `scene(_:openURLContexts:)` | Where a link arrives. Read it for the paths and query items the app understands. | `url` |
| `applinks:` domains in the entitlements, `onContinueUserActivity`, `application(_:continue:restorationHandler:)` | The https links the app takes. They reach it only where its associated domains work. | `url`, always with `expect` |
| `ProcessInfo.processInfo.arguments`, `CommandLine.arguments`, `UserDefaults` keys read at launch, environment variables | A launch argument or variable that starts the app on a screen, or in a state. | `launch` |
| None of these | The app has no way in for this screen. | `run`, with a script that goes there |

## Write routes

```yaml
deeplinks:
  vars:
    base: "myapp://host"
  routes:
    item:
      description: "Open one saved link"
      url: "{base}/item?id={id}&tab={tab}"
      expect: "label~Item {id}"
      params:
        id:  { description: "The link's number" }
        tab: { description: "Which tab to show", values: [notes, preview], optional: true }
    onboarding:
      description: "Start at an onboarding step"
      launch: { args: ["-startRoute", "onboarding/{step}"] }
      preset: fresh-install
      params:
        step: { description: "1 to 4", default: "1" }
    settings/about:
      description: "Tap through to the About screen"
      run: ./scripts/open-about.sh
      expect: "id:about-version"
```

A route has a `description` and exactly one of these three keys, which is its kind:

| Key | What xcd does | Use it when |
|---|---|---|
| `url` | Opens the link on the device. The app is started first if it is not running. | The app handles the link. This is the usual kind. |
| `launch` (`args`, `env`) | Restarts the app with these arguments and environment variables. | The app reads a launch argument, or the state has to be set before the app starts. |
| `run` | Runs the project's script from the worktree root. xcd boots the simulator and leaves the app to the script. | Nothing in the app goes there, or data has to be put in place first. |

The other keys:

| Key | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `params` | The route's parameters. One is required unless it has a `default` or is `optional`. `values` lists the only values allowed. |
| `preset` | A launch preset from `presets:`. The app is restarted with it before the link is sent, so the route gives the same result every time. Not allowed with `run`. |
| `expect` | An element that shows the route arrived. See "Check that it arrived". |
| `timeout` | How long a `run` script may take. The default is `5m`. |

- **Names** group with `/`, like `settings/about`.
- **`{name}`** stands for a parameter or a var. It is read in `url`, in `launch` args and env values, and in `expect`. It is not read in `run`: a script gets its values as environment variables.
- **Encoding.** In a `url`, a parameter's value is percent-encoded, so it cannot change the link's structure. `raw: true` on a parameter inserts its value as given. Use it for a value that is already encoded or that holds several path segments, and know that such a value can change the link's structure.
- **An optional parameter** may only be the whole value of a query item, like `tab={tab}`. The item is left out when no value is given.
- **`vars`** hold text that routes share, like the scheme and host. `.xcdev.local.yml` can override one, which points every route at another host on this machine:

```yaml
deeplinks:
  vars:
    base: "myapp-dev://staging"
```

A mistake in a route, such as an unknown placeholder, two kinds, or an unknown preset, is reported when the config loads. After editing, run `xcd open` and `xcd open <route> --dry-run`. `xcd config explain deeplinks` documents every key.

## Check that it arrived

Sending a link only means it was sent. `expect` names an element that shows the route worked, in a form `xcd ui wait` takes: `id:<identifier>`, `label:<text>`, or `label~<part>`. After the delivery xcd waits up to 10 seconds for it.

- **Give every https route an `expect`.** An https link reaches the app only where the app's associated domains work on that simulator. Otherwise Safari opens it, and the simulator still reports success. With `expect` the command fails with `wait_timeout` and says the app is in the background.
- **Name something specific to the parameters**, like `"label~Item {id}"`. If the element is already on screen when a link goes to the running app, the check proves nothing, and the result says `check: inconclusive`. It says the same when xcd could not look at the app before the link was sent, for example because the app was in the background.
- **The check is skipped**, and the result says so, for a Mac app and for a simulator that is not an iPhone or iPad on iOS 17 or later.
- **A link given directly** has no check. Follow `xcd open '<url>'` with `xcd ui wait <target>`.

## Warm and cold starts

A link that arrives while the app is not running takes a different path through the app than one that arrives while it is up, and that path is where link bugs tend to be.

- `xcd open <route>` sends the link to the running app. If the app is not running, xcd starts it and then sends the link.
- `xcd open <route> --cold` stops the app and lets the link itself start it.
- On a simulator, `--cold` cannot be combined with a preset: the system starts the app, so launch arguments cannot be passed. What the app prints is not captured for that run; its log messages are still in `xcd logs`. On a Mac both work.
- An app that attaches its link handler late, after a sign-in or a loading screen, can miss a link sent right after launch, exactly as it would miss a real link that started it. For such an app run `xcd launch`, then `xcd ui wait` for something on the first real screen, then `xcd open <route>`.

## Script routes

The script runs with `/bin/sh -c` from the worktree root, and its output goes to a log that the result names. It gets the worktree's context (`xcd context` prints it) and these:

| Variable | Holds |
|---|---|
| `XCD_ROUTE` | The route's name. |
| `XCD_PARAM_<NAME>` | One per parameter that has a value, with the name in capitals. An optional parameter with no value is unset. |

```sh
#!/bin/sh
# scripts/open-about.sh
set -eu
xcd launch --device "$XCD_DEVICE"
xcd ui tap "label:Settings" --device "$XCD_DEVICE"
xcd ui tap "label:About" --device "$XCD_DEVICE"
```

- Pass `--device "$XCD_DEVICE"` to every `xcd` command in the script. Built-in commands do not read `XCD_DEVICE`.
- No build is needed before a script route, so the script may build or install. `XCD_APP_PATH` and `XCD_BUNDLE_ID` are unset when nothing is built.
- The script may open another route with `xcd open`. That route does not get this one's parameters.
- A non-zero exit is `route_failed`, with the last lines of the log. Running past `timeout` is `timeout`.
- `--preset` and `--cold` do not apply, because the script starts the app itself.

## Mac apps

`xcd open --device mac` sends the link to this worktree's build of the app, in the background. The frontmost app does not change, and another worktree's copy of the app does not get the link. An https link is handed to the app as an opened URL (`onOpenURL`, `application(_:open:)`). It does not arrive as a universal-link user activity, so an app that only handles `NSUserActivity` will not see it.

## When something fails

Every failure ends with `next:`. Do what it says first.

| Error | Meaning | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| `usage` | An unknown route, a missing or unknown parameter, a value outside `values`, or a scheme the built app does not register. | The error lists the route's parameters, or the schemes the app registers. |
| `config_invalid` | A route in `.xcdev.yml` breaks a rule. | The error names the key. `xcd config explain deeplinks` documents it. |
| `app_not_built` | There is no build to deliver to. `xcd open` never builds. | `xcd run`. |
| `app_crashed` | The app ended in the seconds after the delivery. | Read the crash report in the error. |
| `wait_timeout` | The route's `expect` did not appear. | The error lists what is on screen. "The app is in the background" means the link opened somewhere else. |
| `route_failed` | A script exited non-zero, or a `--cold` link did not start the app. | Read the script's log, or `xcd logs`. |
| `runner_failed` | The arrival check could not run. The link had already been sent. | `xcd ui stop`, retry, then `xcd doctor`. |
"""#

/// docs/agents/guides/setup.md
private let guideSetup = #"""
# Set up xcd in a project

Use this guide when a project has no `.xcdev.yml`, or when the user wants to change how xcd builds, tests, and runs it. You run the commands and talk to the user; xcd never prompts.

## Steps

1. Run `xcd init --plan`. It detects the project, its schemes and platforms, and the simulators and Xcode versions on this Mac, then prints the decisions to make. It writes nothing in the project. Add `--json` to get `{detected, decisions: [{key, ask, options, recommended, reason}]}`. It reads the project files, so it usually takes a second or two. If they don't say which platform the app builds for (say, SDKROOT is set by an expression), it asks xcodebuild instead, using Swift packages Xcode or `xcd build` already resolved for the project; only when there are none does xcodebuild fetch them, which can take a minute. xcd removes the empty folders xcodebuild creates in the project, but when xcodebuild has to resolve packages for a project that has no Package.resolved, it may add one.
2. Ask the user one decision at a time. Lead with the recommendation and its reason, for example: "Which iPhone should agents build and test on by default? I recommend iPhone 18 Pro, the newest installed." The `ask` text can be relayed as-is.
3. Skip any decision the user wants left at the default: don't pass it to `--set`, and it gets its recommendation.
4. When the user asks what a setting means, run `xcd config explain <key>` (for example `xcd config explain xcode`) and answer from it. `xcd config explain` with no key lists every key.
5. Apply the answers with `xcd init --set <key>=<value> …`. Quote values that contain spaces: `xcd init --set devices.default="iPhone 17 Pro" xcode=system`. To take every recommendation, run `xcd init --defaults`. If a `.xcdev.yml` already exists, add `--force` to replace it.
6. Finish with `xcd config show`, and check that it matches what the user chose. Then run `xcd doctor`: it checks that the configured Xcode, device types, and runtimes are installed, and every problem it reports says what to do.

Commit `.xcdev.yml`. `xcd init` adds `.xcdev/` (per-worktree build state) and `.xcdev.local.yml` (per-machine overrides) to `.gitignore`.

If the project has a `.swiftlint.yml` that would let SwiftLint search `.xcdev/`, `xcd init` also adds `.xcdev` to its `excluded` list and says so; commit that change too. SwiftLint searches hidden folders, so without the entry every build that runs it also lints the build folder and the package checkouts, which can add minutes. When `xcd init` reports this as a `warning:` instead, it left the file alone: add the entry by hand, or run `xcd doctor --fix`. A project that starts using SwiftLint later gets the same message as a `note:` in the output of `xcd build`, `xcd run`, and `xcd test`.

## What each section of .xcdev.yml is for

| Section | What it controls | Change it when |
|---|---|---|
| `project` | The .xcworkspace, .xcodeproj, or Package.swift that xcd builds. | The repo has several, or the project moved. |
| `xcode` | Which Xcode builds this project. Absent means the Xcode selected with xcode-select. | The project needs a specific Xcode version. |
| `schemes` | The schemes `xcd build`/`run` (`app`) and `xcd test` (`test`) use. | The project has several app or test schemes. |
| `defaultDevice` | The device commands use without `--device`. | Most work targets a different device. |
| `devices` | Named simulators (and `mac`). Each worktree gets its own clone of each, made on first use. | The app needs another device type, a paired watch, or a pinned runtime. |
| `presets` | Named launch arguments and environment for `xcd run --preset`. | The app has launch modes agents reuse. |
| `worktrees` | Where `xcd wt create` puts new worktrees. | The default `../<project>.worktrees` doesn't suit the repo. |
| `logs` | `maxFollow`, the longest `xcd logs --follow` may stream. | Agents need longer log streams. |
| `ui` | Where screenshots and recordings go (`media.dir`) and how long they can record (`record.maxDuration`). | The app needs a custom capture folder or different recording limits. |
| `commands` | Project commands, run as `xcd <name>`. | The project has scripts agents should run with the worktree's context. |
| `hooks` | Scripts run at `post-worktree-create`, `pre-build`, `post-launch`, and `pre-worktree-remove`. | Worktrees need bootstrapping, code generation, or permissions after launch. |
| `deeplinks` | The links the app can open, by name (`routes`), and text they share (`vars`). `xcd open` lists and opens them (`xcd guide deeplinks`). | The app handles links or launch arguments that take an agent straight to a screen. |

Per-machine overrides go in `.xcdev.local.yml`, which is merged over `.xcdev.yml`.
"""#

/// docs/agents/guides/ui.md
private let guideUi = #"""
# See and drive the app's UI

Use this guide to check your work in the running app: read what is on screen, act on it, and confirm what happened. It works on iPhone and iPad simulators running iOS 17 or later. Nothing here opens Device Hub (Simulator.app before Xcode 27) or takes focus on the Mac; when a person wants to watch, `xcd sim open` shows them the simulator.

## The loop

1. Start the app with `xcd run` (build, install, launch) or `xcd launch`. Actions need the app in front.
2. See: `xcd ui tree` lists the elements on screen. Each row has a ref (`e1`, `e2`, …), a role, the label, the value, the identifier, and the frame in points. `xcd ui screenshot` saves a PNG and prints its path, for what the tree can't show: layout, colour, images.
3. Act: tap, type, or swipe (see Actions).
4. Verify: add `--tree` to the action to get the tree as it is afterwards, or run `xcd ui wait <target>`. An action that succeeds only means the gesture was sent. It does not mean the app reacted.

```sh
xcd run
xcd ui tree
xcd ui type "me@example.com" --into id:email
xcd ui tap "label:Sign In" --tree
xcd ui wait "label~Welcome" --timeout 10
```

## Targets

A target is one argument. Quote it when it has spaces.

| Form | Matches |
|---|---|
| `id:<identifier>` | The accessibility identifier, exactly. Prefer this: it doesn't change with the app's language. |
| `label:<text>` | The label, exactly. |
| `label~<text>` | Part of the label, in any case. |
| `ref:e<N>` | A row of the newest tree. |
| `@x,y` | A point, in points (tap and long-press only). Nothing is checked: it taps whatever is there. |

When several elements match, the error lists them as `e1`, `e2`, …. Those become the refs, so `ref:e2` picks one. `--role button` or `--index 0` also narrow a match. Refs are good until the next tree: `xcd ui tree`, an action with `--tree`, and an "ambiguous" error each replace them.

## Actions

| Command | What it does |
|---|---|
| `xcd ui tap <target>` | Taps the element's centre. |
| `xcd ui long-press <target> [--duration 2s]` | Presses and holds (default 1s). |
| `xcd ui type <text> [--into <target>] [--clear] [--submit]` | Types into the field (`--into` taps it first), or into whatever has focus. `--clear` replaces what the field holds. `--submit` presses Return. |
| `xcd ui swipe up\|down\|left\|right [--on <target>]` | Swipes from the centre of the element, or of the screen. The direction is the way the finger moves, so `up` scrolls a list towards its end. |
| `xcd ui swipe --from x,y --to x,y` | Swipes between two points. |
| `xcd ui button home\|action\|camera` | Presses a hardware button. `home` works everywhere; the others only on devices that have them. |
| `xcd ui rotate portrait\|landscape-left\|landscape-right\|portrait-upside-down` | Rotates the device. |
| `xcd ui wait <target> [--gone] [--timeout 10s]` | Waits for an element to be on screen, or to be gone. |

An action on an element waits up to `--timeout` (default 2s; 10s for `wait`) for it to exist and be reachable. Every action except `wait` takes `--tree`, which prints the tree as it is afterwards. The app is the one from the last build; `--bundle <id>` picks another, and `--system` acts on the Home Screen.

## Things to know

- **Getting to a screen.** When the project has routes, `xcd open <route>` goes there in one step, and it keeps working when the screens in between change. `xcd open` lists the routes, and `xcd guide deeplinks` explains them.
- **System alerts** (permission prompts) are listed at the end of the tree, marked `system`. While one is up, nothing in the app can be acted on: tap one of the alert's buttons first.
- **The keyboard** covers the lower part of the screen, and an element under it is refused. Scroll the element up with `xcd ui swipe up`, or dismiss the keyboard first: `xcd ui type "" --submit` presses Return, which closes it in most single-line fields (and submits the field), or tap the keyboard's own Return or Done key (`xcd ui tree --all` lists the keys).
- **Off-screen elements** are not in the default tree. `xcd ui tree --all` lists them, marked `offscreen`. Swipe, then read the tree again.
- **Typing** uses the software keyboard. If none comes up, the text is pasted instead, which replaces the device's pasteboard; the report says `by keyboard` or `by paste`. Check the field's value in the tree afterwards. With nothing focused there is nowhere to type, and the command fails: pass `--into <target>`.
- **A crash** during an action is reported as `app_crashed`, with the crash report when one was written.
- **The first `xcd ui` command** builds a small runner once per Xcode (about 30 seconds) and starts it on your simulator. On a brand-new simulator the start can take minutes. Later commands take about a second.
- **`xcd test`** stops the runner when the test run includes UI tests, since both would drive the same device. The next `xcd ui` command starts it again.
- **Device state** is set with `xcd sim`: `xcd sim open-url <url>`, `xcd sim push --alert <text>`, `xcd sim privacy grant photos`, `xcd sim pasteboard set --text <text>` or `--image <file>`, `xcd sim appearance dark`.
- Add `--json` to any command for one JSON object: the tree's elements, or an action's `element`, `x`, `y`, and `tree`.

## When something fails

Every failure ends with `next:`. Do what it says first.

| Error | Meaning | What to do |
|---|---|---|
| `element_not_found` | Nothing matched in time, or the app isn't in front. | Read `xcd ui tree`; the error names the closest labels and identifiers. |
| `element_not_hittable` | It exists but is off screen, disabled, or covered. | Scroll it into view, or deal with the alert or keyboard over it. |
| `element_ambiguous` | Several elements matched. | Pick one with `ref:e<N>`, `--index`, or `--role`. |
| `stale_ref` | The ref is from an older tree. | Run `xcd ui tree` and use a new ref. |
| `wait_timeout` | The element didn't appear (or go) in time. | The error lists what is on screen. Check the app did what you expected. |
| `app_crashed` | The action ended the app. | Read the crash report in the error, fix the crash, then `xcd run`. |
| `runner_failed` | The runner didn't start or stopped answering. | `xcd ui stop`, retry, then `xcd doctor`. |
"""#

enum GuideText {
    static let topics: [String: String] = [
